# Target C — terminal FREE currency forces a linear rhombus-count deficit

**Date:** 2026-09-18  
**Status:** proof-only synthesis; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** full-core Type-II survivor sequences carrying a non-anchored all-inactive FREE reflection module and satisfying `pi D-q=o(D)`  
**Depends on:** `target_c_free_terminal_train_face_bridge_2026-09-18.md`, canonical Sections 67/77/85, and the same audited-working all-defect phase/train gap used by the train/face bridge  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_free_terminal_rhombus_deficit_floor_2026_09_18.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The terminal FREE train/face bridge still ends with an apparent two-way escape: either
`(q-k)/D` is very large or the exterior isolate population `Z_ext/D` is large.  The
full-source Oler count removes that escape.  Exterior isolates consume the same
second-order population budget as missing strict-rhombus faces, so the two branches
collapse to one linear rhombus-count deficit.

Put

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\qquad
s_2:=\sqrt{\Delta_2},
\qquad
\mathcal D_4:=\Delta_2D^2-R_4.
\]

The main consequence is

\[
\boxed{
\mathcal D_4
\ge
\left(
{1\over10}+{6\over5}s_2-o(1)
\right)D
+{4\over5}Z_{\rm ext}.
}
\tag{RD.1}
\]

Numerically,

\[
{1\over10}+{6\over5}s_2
=
1.242775368236749\ldots .
\tag{RD.2}
\]

Thus every terminal FREE survivor in this scope has

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {\Delta_2D^2-R_4\over D}
\ge1.242775368236749\ldots .
}
\tag{RD.3}
\]

This more than doubles the older proof-only `0.586...--0.746...` scale of the
second-order rhombus deficit.  More importantly, (RD.1) prices any surviving exterior
population on top of the `1.242775...D` baseline instead of leaving `Z_ext` as an
independent recipient.

The original terminal FREE metric law also gives

\[
\lambda_D+Z_{\rm ext}\ge {D\over2}-o(D),
\qquad
\lambda_D:=b-\operatorname{per}(F).
\]

Combining it with (RD.1) yields the weighted structural payment

\[
\boxed{
\mathcal D_4+{4\over5}\lambda_D
\ge
\left(
{1\over2}+{6\over5}s_2-o(1)
\right)D.
}
\tag{RD.4}
\]

The coefficient is

\[
{1\over2}+{6\over5}s_2
=1.642775368236749\ldots .
\tag{RD.5}
\]

Consequently at least one of the actual outer-walk detour and the absolute
strict-rhombus count deficit is linear at the explicit scale

\[
\boxed{
\max\{\mathcal D_4,\lambda_D\}
\ge
(0.912652982353749\ldots-o(1))D.
}
\tag{RD.6}
\]

No comparison of local chamber Oler slacks with the full-hull Oler slack is used.

---

## 1. Exact full-source population bridge

Let `M` be the positive-contact-core population and retain the canonical full-core
notation:

- `R_4` is the number of bounded strict unit-rhombus faces;
- `f` is the number of bounded nonquadrilateral contact faces;
- `q=|B|` is the loaded force-hull vertex count;
- `k=d_H+s_Z`;
- `Z_ch` is the contact-isolated population inside `F`;
- `Z_ext` is the source population outside `F`.

Canonical face counting gives the exact identity

\[
M=R_4+f+q-k+1.
\tag{RD.7}
\]

The full source is

\[
n=M+Z_{ch}+Z_{\rm ext}.
\tag{RD.8}
\]

Oler on the source hull, followed by the isodiametric and Cauchy
perimeter-diameter bounds, gives

\[
n\le
\Delta_2D^2+{\pi\over2}D+1.
\tag{RD.9}
\]

Substituting (RD.7)--(RD.8) into (RD.9) and cancelling the two `+1` terms gives the
finite inequality

\[
\boxed{
\mathcal D_4
\ge
(q-k)+f+Z_{ch}+Z_{\rm ext}-{\pi\over2}D.
}
\tag{RD.10}
\]

This is the key accounting step.  It retains the exterior source population instead
of discarding it as in the older absolute rhombus-deficit estimates.

---

## 2. The all-defect gap converts population into a `t/2+z` cost

Normalize

\[
t_D:={q-k\over D},
\qquad
z_D:={Z_{\rm ext}\over D}.
\]

On the terminal branch `q/D->pi`, hence

\[
{k\over D}=\pi-t_D+o(1).
\tag{RD.11}
\]

The audited-working all-defect phase/train theorem used by the preceding terminal
FREE train/face bridge states

\[
2f-k\ge(\gamma-o(1))D,
\tag{RD.12}
\]

with

\[
\gamma
=2s_2-{\pi\over2}
=0.333829286933018\ldots .
\tag{RD.13}
\]

Equations (RD.11)--(RD.12) imply

\[
{f\over D}
\ge
{\pi-t_D+\gamma\over2}-o(1).
\tag{RD.14}
\]

Drop the nonnegative `Z_ch` term from (RD.10), divide by `D`, and insert (RD.14):

\[
\boxed{
{\mathcal D_4\over D}
\ge
{t_D\over2}+z_D+{\gamma\over2}-o(1).
}
\tag{RD.15}
\]

So both a large high-`t` value and a large exterior population increase the
rhombus-count deficit.

---

## 3. Insert the terminal FREE train/face bridge

The immediately preceding train/face theorem proves

\[
\boxed{
t_D+{2\over5}z_D
\ge
C_{\rm FREE}-o(1),
}
\tag{RD.16}
\]

where

\[
C_{\rm FREE}
={\pi\over2}+{1\over5}+{2\over5}s_2
=2.151721449540479\ldots .
\tag{RD.17}
\]

Rewrite the right side of (RD.15) as

\[
{t_D\over2}+z_D+{\gamma\over2}
=
{1\over2}\left(t_D+{2\over5}z_D\right)
+{4\over5}z_D+{\gamma\over2}.
\]

Therefore

\[
{\mathcal D_4\over D}
\ge
{C_{\rm FREE}+\gamma\over2}
+{4\over5}z_D-o(1).
\tag{RD.18}
\]

The constants simplify exactly:

\[
{C_{\rm FREE}+\gamma\over2}
=
{1\over10}+{6\over5}s_2.
\tag{RD.19}
\]

This proves (RD.1)--(RD.3).

The exterior population is therefore no longer a cheap alternative to the high-`t`
branch.  Every additional `cD` exterior isolates require at least another
`0.8cD` units of absolute rhombus-count deficit.

---

## 4. Return to the actual outer-walk detour

The terminal FREE boundary-crossing theorem also proves

\[
\lambda_D+Z_{\rm ext}
\ge {D\over2}-o(D).
\tag{RD.20}
\]

Add `(4/5)lambda_D` to (RD.1) and use (RD.20):

\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal D_4+{4\over5}\lambda_D
&\ge
\left({1\over10}+{6\over5}s_2-o(1)\right)D
+{4\over5}(Z_{\rm ext}+\lambda_D)\\
&\ge
\left({1\over2}+{6\over5}s_2-o(1)\right)D.
\end{aligned}
\]

This is (RD.4).

If both `mathcal D_4` and `lambda_D` were below `xD+o(D)`, the left side of (RD.4)
would be below `(9/5)xD+o(D)`.  Hence

\[
x\ge
{5\over9}\left({1\over2}+{6\over5}s_2\right)
=0.912652982353749\ldots,
\]

which proves (RD.6).

Two useful endpoints are:

\[
\lambda_D=o(D)
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\mathcal D_4
\ge(1.642775368236749\ldots-o(1))D,
\tag{RD.21}
\]

and

\[
Z_{\rm ext}=o(D)
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\mathcal D_4
\ge(1.242775368236749\ldots-o(1))D.
\tag{RD.22}
\]

At the formal scalar equality point of the preceding FREE train/face relaxation,

\[
t=C_{\rm FREE},\qquad
z=0,\qquad
{f\over D}={Q\over D}=0.661850245491166\ldots,
\qquad
{\lambda_D\over D}={1\over2},
\]

and (RD.15) is also tight at

\[
{\mathcal D_4\over D}=1.242775368236749\ldots .
\tag{RD.23}
\]

Thus the coefficient in (RD.3) is sharp for the current relaxed scalar system.  A
larger coefficient requires genuinely new geometry, not another rearrangement of the
same scalar inequalities.

---

## 5. Price inside the old high-`t` window

The train/face bridge gives

\[
z_D\ge
{3\pi+1+\gamma-5t_D\over2}-o(1).
\tag{RD.24}
\]

Substituting into (RD.18) gives a stronger rhombus-deficit price whenever `t_D` lies
below `C_FREE`.  For example:

\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
T & t_D\le T & \liminf \mathcal D_4/D\\ \hline
1.8440 & Z_{\rm ext}/D\ge0.769303623851198 & 1.858218267317707\\
1.8687 & Z_{\rm ext}/D\ge0.707553623851198 & 1.808818267317707\\
2.0000 & Z_{\rm ext}/D\ge0.379303623851198 & 1.546218267317708\\
C_{\rm FREE} & Z_{\rm ext}/D\ge0 & 1.242775368236749
\end{array}
\tag{RD.25}
\]

Thus a terminal FREE survivor remaining inside the complete September-17
`t<=1.8687` support-normal-form window must pay more than `1.8088D` in absolute
strict-rhombus count deficit.

---

## 6. Geometric form

Let

\[
\delta_F:={\pi D^2\over4}-\operatorname{area}(F).
\]

Canonical Section 77 decomposes the force hull into strict-rhombus faces and inactive
chambers.  If

\[
E_4:=\sum_R(\operatorname{area}(R)-a_0),
\qquad
a_0={\sqrt3\over2},
\]

then exactly

\[
\boxed{
a_0\mathcal D_4
=\delta_F+A_{ch}+E_4.
}
\tag{RD.26}
\]

Multiplying (RD.1) by `a_0` therefore gives

\[
\boxed{
\delta_F+A_{ch}+E_4
\ge
(1.076275040090585\ldots-o(1))D
+{2\sqrt3\over5}Z_{\rm ext}.
}
\tag{RD.27}
\]

Since

\[
{2\sqrt3\over5}=0.692820323027551\ldots,
\]

exterior population now has an explicit geometric area-defect price.

Likewise (RD.4) becomes

\[
\boxed{
\delta_F+A_{ch}+E_4
+{2\sqrt3\over5}\lambda_D
\ge
(1.422685201604360\ldots-o(1))D.
}
\tag{RD.28}
\]

This is the form most directly aligned with the remaining global packing and detour
ledgers.

---

## 7. Scope and remaining bottleneck

The new argument does not prove a contradiction.  It changes the remaining terminal
FREE obstruction from an uncontrolled source-population branch into a second-order
structural deficit:

```text
terminal all-inactive FREE module
  -> train/face bridge t + (2/5) z >= 2.1517214495...
  -> full-source Oler keeps the z term instead of discarding it
  -> absolute rhombus deficit >= 1.2427753682... D + 0.8 Z_ext
  -> together with lambda+Z_ext >= D/2:
       rhombus deficit + 0.8 lambda >= 1.6427753682... D.
```

The exact next target is therefore an upper budget for either side of (RD.28), or a
linear-order phase/attachment-cell theorem that cannot tolerate

\[
\Delta_2D^2-R_4\ge(1.2427753682\ldots-o(1))D.
\]

That is a better-defined seam than bounding `Z_ext` directly.  Existing Type-II
arguments control `R_4` only at quadratic leading order, so they do not yet contradict
this new first-order deficit.  A useful next run should try to transfer the deficit in
(RD.26) into the Section-122/123 attachment-cell detour budget or obtain a first-order
lower bound on `R_4` strong enough to oppose (RD.3).

**Disposition:** exterior population eliminated as an independent terminal FREE
recipient; proved the `1.242775...D + 0.8 Z_ext` rhombus-deficit floor and the
`1.642775...D` weighted rhombus/detour payment.  The authoritative theorem-state ledger
is unchanged; C-II remains open.